[White "24. King's Gambit with 3.Nf3"]
[Black "King's Gambit 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.d3 #01"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit was the most popular opening of the Romantic Era of chess. Nearly all the best players of the 19th century played it with both colours, producing a number of highly interesting and spectacular games. However, since the positional school chess came to the fore, its popularity has been steadily declining. In the recent decade, modern computers dealt an additional blow to the King's Gambit's reputation, showing that White's compensation is hardly sufficient when faced with precise play from Black. White will struggle to even equalize. Nevertheless, one will occasionally face a King's Gambit aficionado or an opponent trying to use the opening as a surprise weapon, so it is important to be at least basically prepared for it. -KEY- } exf4 3. Nf3 Nf6 $5 ( { The Schallop Defence, which is the fifth most popular of Black's replies. The basis for this recommendation is simple. Compared to } 3... g5 { or } d6 { Black does not need to learn many variations here and still achieves an excellent position in all of the lines. } ) 
4. d3 { Too passive to be good. } d5 5. e5 $2 { White can hardly combine passive and active play. } Nh5 $19 { Black enjoys an improved version of the 4.e5 Nh5 line, which means that White is in great trouble. Objectively Black might be even already winning with the best play for both sides e.g. } 6. Be2 g5 7. Nxg5 Qxg5 8. Bxh5 Qh4+ 9. Kf1 Nc6 10. Bf3 Be6 { Black was winning in Mishuchkov-Varavin, Moscow 1991 } { _ } 
