1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit was the most popular opening of the Romantic Era of chess. Nearly all the best players of the 19th century played it with both colours, producing a number of highly interesting and spectacular games. However, since the positional school chess came to the fore, its popularity has been steadily declining. In the recent decade, modern computers dealt an additional blow to the King's Gambit's reputation, showing that White's compensation is hardly sufficient when faced with precise play from Black. White will struggle to even equalize. Nevertheless, one will occasionally face a King's Gambit aficionado or an opponent trying to use the opening as a surprise weapon, so it is important to be at least basically prepared for it. } exf4 3. Nf3 Nf6 $5 ( { The Schallop Defence, which is the fifth most popular of Black's replies. The basis for this recommendation is simple. Compared to } 3... g5 { or } d6 { Black does not need to learn many variations here and still achieves an excellent position in all of the lines. } ) 
4. e5 Nh5 { The knight may seem awkward on h5, but plays an important role defending the f4 pawn, and, as we will see, after ...g5 it can be transferred via g7 to very good squares, namely e6 or f5. } 5. Be2 g5 6. O-O { -KEY- } h6 $1 { An important move, played in order to always be able to recapture with the pawn in case of Nxg5. } ( 6... Rg8 { is not bad but it doesn't actually transpose to one of 5.d4 and 6.Be2 lines because after } 7. d4 d5 { White can take en passant and in the line mentioned above that's not the case. } ) 
7. d4 d6 $1 ( { More accurate than } 7... d5 { , which would allow } 8. c4 { , where White would achieve a better version of the lines arising after 5.d4 d5. Now however, Black is ready to complete his development and I wasn't able to find any way for White to achieve compensation for the pawn. } ) 
8. Re1 Be7 9. exd6 cxd6 10. Nxg5 hxg5 11. Bxh5 Nc6 { White regained the pawn, but in return Black achieves great play on the kingside thanks to the open h-file and the g5-f4 pawn mass. Black will play ...Bf5, ...Qd7 and actually ...Kf8 might be a good choice as the king will be quite safe here and just cover f7. } 
